TI  - Birth weight, milk production and mortality rate in crossbred cattle (Dwarf taurine x Zebu) in Togo
T2  - JOURNAL OF LIVESTOCK SCIENCE
AB  - Somba cattle is one of the two trypanotolerant West-African Dwarf (WAD) breeds. Crosses between taurine (Bos taurus) and Zebu (Bos indicus) breeds are reported in various localities in West-Africa. The aim of this study was to assess the birth weight, the milk production and the mortality rate in crossbred cattle (Dwarf taurine x Zebu) in Togo. The study was carried out between 2015 and 2017 in February (dry season), May and September (rainy season) of each year in two Prefectures (Mo and Tchaoudjo) located in Central Administrative region of Togo. A total of 207 calves and 290 milking cows were randomly sampled for the study. Calves consisted of 72 WAD, 67 Zebu and 68 crossbred (WAD x Zebu) phenotypically taurine. The male calves weigh 17.70 +/- 2,6 kg against 16.59 +/- 1.97 kg for the female in crossbreeds; 13.76 +/- 1.07 kg and 12.37 +/- 1.16 kg in WAD and 22.55 +/- 3.34 kg and 19.66 +/- 2.15 kg in Zebus cattle. The milk production was significantly higher in the crossbreed (0.68 +/- 0.27 L) than in WAD (0.46 +/- 0.15 L) on the one hand and it was influenced by the rank of calving (P = 0.000) on the other hand. The mortality rates were 10.98 +/- 3.41%; 11.85 +/- 4.19% and 12.17 +/- 5.06% in Crossbred, WAD and zebus respectively and no significant difference was observed among the three phenotypes. However, the calving-season had significant effect (P = 0.003) on the mortality rates. In the light of the foregoing figures, we come to conclusion that Somba breed performs far less compared to the crossbreed (Dwarf taurine x Zebu) and Zebus phenotypes in terms of birth weight and milk production.
