TI  - TNFα Altered Inflammatory Responses, Impaired Health and Productivity, but Did Not Affect Glucose or Lipid Metabolism in Early-Lactation Dairy Cows
T2  - PLOS ONE
AB  - Inflammation may be a major contributing factor to peripartum metabolic disorders in dairy cattle. We tested whether administering an inflammatory cytokine, recombinant bovine tumor necrosis factor-alpha (rbTNF alpha), affects milk production, metabolism, and health during this period. Thirty-three Holstein cows (9 primiparous and 24 multiparous) were randomly assigned to 1 of 3 treatments at parturition. Treatments were 0 (Control), 1.5, or 3.0 g/kg body weight rbTNF alpha, which were administered once daily by subcutaneous injection for the first 7 days of lactation. Statistical contrasts were used to evaluate the treatment and dose effects of rbTNF alpha administration. Plasma TNF alpha concentrations at 16 h post-administration tended to be increased (P < 0.10) by rbTNF alpha administration, but no dose effect (P > 0.10) was detected; rbTNFa treatments increased (P < 0.01) concentrations of plasma haptoglobin. Most plasma eicosanoids were not affected (P > 0.10) by rbTNF alpha administration, but 6 out of 16 measured eicosanoids changed (P < 0.05) over the first week of lactation, reflecting elevated inflammatory mediators in the days immediately following parturition. Dry matter and water intake, milk yield, and milk fat and protein yields were all decreased (P < 0.05) by rbTNFa treatments by 15 to 18%. Concentrations of plasma glucose, insulin, beta-hydroxybutyrate, non-esterified fatty acids, triglyceride, 3-methylhistidine,and liver triglyceride were unaffected (P < 0.10) by rbTNFa treatment. Glucose turnover rate was unaffected (P = 0.18) by rbTNFa administration. The higher dose of rbTNFa tended to increase the risk of cows developing one or more health disorders (P = 0.08). Taken together, these results indicate that administration of rbTNFa daily for the first 7 days of lactation altered inflammatory responses, impaired milk production and health, but did not significantly affect liver triglyceride accumulation or nutrient metabolism in dairy cows.
