TI  - Preliminary study on the major important camel calf diseases and other factors causing calf mortality in the Somali regional state of Ethiopia
T2  - PROCEEDINGS OF THE INTERNATIONAL CAMEL CONFERENCE: RECENT TRENDS IN CAMELIDS RESEARCH AND FUTURE STRATEGIES FOR SAVING CAMELS
CP  - International Camel Conference
AB  - The study was undertaken in the 5 randomly selected districts of Afder zone. Out of 8 districts 5 districts were selected in which 15 households were selected from each districts and totally 75 household were selected. Random sampling technique was used. Calf mortality was seen as prenatal death due to abortion, postnatal death from first week to 3 months of calf age and before weaning period. The latter was mainly caused due to some endemic diseases and other associated factors. In this study, abortion rate was 16% and it was caused by several factors. These include accidental death of foetus and trypanosomiasis, which contributed 64.3 and 28.6%, respectively, in the case of Jarati, while trypanosomiasis and stress conditions contributed 40 and 46.7%, respectively in the case of Hargelle. On the other hand, stress conditions caused by adverse environmental conditions and unidentified poisonous plants, contributed 26.7 and 73.3%, respectively in the case of Barey. Similarly, trypanosomiasis, accidental death and stress conditions and browsing of poisonous plants contributed 33.3, 40.0, 20.0 and 6.7%, respectively, in the case of Dollo-Bay. With regard to El-kari district, about 66.7, 26.7 and 6.7% of respondents claimed that abortion was caused by accidental deaths, poisonous plants and stressful conditions, respectively. On the other hand, calf death was very high during the first week after birth. About 60, 50, 55, 45, 35% of Hargelle, Jarati, Barey, Dollo-Bay and El-kari, respectively, suggested that on an average 51% of calf losses were encountered during the first week of calves. Meanwhile, calf mortality of about 30% was encountered during first 90 days of calf age; where as the remaining 19% were encountered after 90 days of calf life, before weaning. Poor colostrum feeding practice is also believed to be one of the major causes of the calf mortality during the first week of calves life. Further more, some endemic diseases and other associated factors were also reported to be amongst the major causes of calf mortality during the lactation period before weaning. The most important disease found was called calf scour (Daab). The morbidity and mortality rate of calf scour was 87 and 39%, respectively. Sunken eye (Ilqod) was considered as the second problematic disease of calves by herders. The disease caused serious economic losses to the households through loss of milk after death of the calves. The morbidity and mortality rate due to sunken eyes were 57 and 12%, respectively. Contagious ecthyma (Canbaruur) was considered as the one of the important diseases of calves by herders. The morbidity and mortality rate of contagious ecthyma was 75 and 6.9% respectively. Contagious necrotic skin was also considered as the one of the important diseases of calves by herders. About 88% in all districts reported that the disease affected their calves with morbidity and mortality rate of calf 35 and 4.6%, respectively. Other endemic diseases reported were trypanosomiasis with morbidity and mortality rate of 9.6 and 6.7%. Camel pox had morbidity and mortality rates of 42 and 7%, respectively and pneumonia with mortality rate of 7%. On the other hand, factors causing calf losses included predator about 4.8, 23.8, 26.6, 16.7, and 26.2% of Hargelle, Jarati, Barey, Dollo-Bay and El-kari, respectively suggested that predators are considired next to diseases in causing calf mortatity.
