TI  - INVESTIGATION OF THE LUTEOTROPIC ACTION OF 15-GLYCAL CLOPROSTENOL (GLYSTROPHAN INJ SPOFA) AND ITS RESIDUES IN THE MILK AND ORGANS OF CATTLE
T2  - BIOLOGIZACE A CHEMIZACE ZIVOCISNE VYROBY-VETERINARIA
AB  - The action of 15-glycal cloprostenol on isolated luteum tissue and on the corpus luteum was studied after administration in vivo. Residues of the product were determined in milk and tissues and the possibility of prevention of early embryonal mortality was experimentally verified. The tested substance was added at concentrations of 2, 5, 25 and 250 .mu.g per ml to the isolated luteal tissue in the culivation medium. In two hours, 200-286% of the progesterone, recorded in the control group, was  excreted into the medium with a supplement of 15-glycal cloprostenol; the largest amount was observed to be excreted in the group given 25 .mu.g per ml. This group had the largest number of cells and the highest nucleocytoplasmatic ratio after 24 h. In the corpus luteum of the cows killed the 5th day (9th day of the cycle) after administration of 0.5 mg of 15-glycal cloprostenol (Glystrophan inj. Spofa), the cell had a larger average area and the nucleoplasmatic ratio was higher than in the  control group. The residues were determined by RIA after i.m. administration of a dose of 0.5 mg pro toto. Eight hours after administration, the average values in the milk of cows in the experimental group did not differ from the natural background of the control group. Seventy-two hours after administration, the contents of residues in the muscle, liver and kidneys of the test animals were higher by less than 20 pg per g, compared with the residue levels in the natural background. No attempt  was made to see whether the residue was a metabolite or the substance administered. Values lower than in the control group were recorded in the 96th hour. The possibility of using the luteotropic action was tested in herds of cows with low pregnancy rates where embryonal mortality was assumed to be high. The tested substance was administered in the preparation Glystrophan inj. Spofa at a rate of 0.5 mg (2 ml) i.m. as a single treatmemnt the 4th-6th day after oestrus. The pregnancy rate of the  604 control cows was 43.5%, whereas in the 804 treated cows the pregnancy rate was 55.2%, with a high variability between the trials. The difference between the control and experimental groups was -4.4 .+-. 17.6%, the average being +11.7% in favor of the experimental groups.
